recognise as many technical indicators (or patterns) as possible.
We present ﬁnancial markets in Section (2) and discuss the properties of asset returns, in particular the long range
dependence (LRD) and the difﬁculties of measuring it empirically. Then, we introduce neural networks and focus on
artiﬁcial neural networks (ANN) and recurrent neural networks (RNN). We brieﬂy discuss the learning process and
consider prediction tasks for p1q chaotic series prediction (regression problem), p2q autonomous pattern generation
(classiﬁcation problem). In Section (3), we test neural networks on the Mackey-Glass signal and several real-time
ﬁnancial time series in order to gain some insight on their capability of reproducing the dynamics of ﬁnancial time
series. We choose to model transient processes with Reservoir Computing (RC), which is an approach designed to train
and analyse RNNs in a fast and efﬁcient way (see Jaeger [2001]). There is no learning in the recurrent connections,
only between the State units and the Output units, and adaptation is based on a simple associative learning mechanism
(see Dominey et al. [2000]). RC models with output feedback were proposed to generate ﬁnancial time series with
interesting properties since feeding the output signal back into the reservoir allows to tackle prediction tasks. In order
to improve the accuracy of the forecast returns, we also consider an online algorithm behaving as an adaptive ﬁlter
throughout the running.
In Section (3.3), we repeat the analysis using Associative Reservoir Computing. Given that an associative connection
between two entities is bidirectional, if the forward relation is many-to-one, the reverse relation (one-to-many) is
ambiguous. This is always the case if there exists several causes resulting in the same effect. More formally, if
there is a non-injective relation between inputs and outputs, there is no unique solution to the inverse problem. This
problem is typical of ﬁnancial time series and traditional feed-forward models can not represent multiple solutions
to a mapping without additional knowledge. In the case of persistent processes we consider solving the ambiguous
problem displayed by market returns by using the Associative Reservoir Computing Theory (ARC) to shape multistable attractor dynamics that reﬂect the multiplicity of solutions (see Reinhart et al. [2009]). To do so, we overwrite
the reservoir forward representation by a joint representation involving input and output. That is, to learn a mapping
from output to input we rely on a feedback matrix to access the latter, and deﬁne a matrix mapping the reservoir states
to the input layer. We then test the model on the Mackey-Glass signal and FX time series.
The prediction of ﬁnancial time series is a highly challenging task since they are multifractal. It requires models to
capture subtle functional relationships among the empirical data even though the underlying relationships are unknown
or hard to describe. Fortunately, such relationships are not required as far as predicting future trend is concerned. This
is because the direction of an underlying asset refers to the movement of the asset price or the trend of ﬂuctuation in
the underlying asset in the future. Therefore, the change of a feature over time is more important than the absolute
value of a feature. Further, forecasting the trends results in networks that are less complex than when forecasting
the price levels. We describe in Section (4) several algorithms for forecasting the directions of the underlying asset.
While accuracy is a good measure for the general ability of a network, it must be combined with return metrics
taking into account the magnitude of the trends that a system discovers. One solution is to consider a framework for
simultaneously forecasting both future price levels and asset price directions. We describe in Section (5) an algorithm
to forecast both market returns and their directions. To conclude, we draw conclusions on the results and deﬁne a set
of rules we want to follow when building our trading algorithm. Since stock prices consist of several patterns, which
appear repeatedly at time intervals, it is logical to focus on learning traditional shapes deﬁned by stock analysts. In
Section (6), we discuss autonomous pattern generation and their prediction in view of forecasting price trends, where
the patterns are distinctive formations of market prices. It is now well known that supervised learning methods are
inadequate for multi-step prediction-learning problems, and that temporal difference (TD) are far preferable. Thus,
we also introduce temporal differences (TD) procedures and discuss how to learn to predict ﬁnancial patterns, which
are multi-step prediction-learning problems. Further, we show how to use TD procedures to infer information about
the magnitude of future market returns.
In Section (7), we use the multifractal formalism (MF) as a framework for testing the capacity of an ML model to
reproduce some statistical properties of time series. We consider a set T of theoretical models Tm, m  1, ..., M,
