Quantitative Analytics
technical indicators (TIs), which are mathematical transformations of market returns (see Appendix (11)). The
most common TIs are the simple moving average (SMA), exponential moving average (EMA), relative strength
index (RSI), rate of change (ROC), moving average convergence / divergence (MACD), William’s oscillator and
average true range (ATR).
In general, academics and practitioners focus on case p1 q, which implies making a statement about the true dynamics (denoted f) followed by market returns. While in case p2 q we only consider part of the probability space, this
approach was originally ignored by academics. However, due to its simplicity of use and the fact that traders are
concerned with the movements of the underlying, this approach has recently gained momentum. From Computational
Learning, an approximation model (the hypothesis, denoted h) must be devised and trained on the full measurement
space (case p1 q) or part of the space (case p2 q). In order to assess if the function h is approximately correct, a
measure of error must be considered (the cost function).
This process requires sophisticated search and examination methods (optimisation algorithms) such as machine learning.
