2.4.5.2
The forecasting horizon
The different forecasting outputs are computed for the following periods: daily,
weekly, monthly, quarterly and yearly. However, an increasing number of articles consider intra-day high frequency
data, which requires highly liquid markets. In that case the data comprises the date and time, open-high-low-close price
levels and the volume traded for each underlying asset. One of the main reason for considering intraday trading horizon
is the notion of self-destruction of predictable patterns in stock prices (see Timmermann et al. [2004]). For the data
to be consistent, historical prices and/or volume are usually adjusted to reﬂect spin-offs, stock splits/consolidations,
stock dividend/bonus and right offerings/entitlement.
2.4.5.3
Alignment
In general, we use one market (such as NASDAQ) as a basis for data alignment and missing
data in other data sources is replaced by linear interpolation.
2.4.5.4
Evaluation methods
Most evaluation methods of the ML models are benchmark models where the proposed improved version is compared against the original one using a forecast error as an evaluation metric. Examples
of benchmark models are buy and hold, random walk, statistical techniques and other machine learning techniques.
We consider a simulated market with trading period T and initial capital C. In a buy and hold strategy, we suppose
that we use all the money to buy stocks on the ﬁrst day and sell the stocks at the end. To do so, we force all possessed
stocks to be cashed at the end of the trading period. The proﬁt is depend on the trend during this testing period,
PnL  C pVT  V0q
V0
where Vt is the value of the portfolio at time t.
We can also assume a trending strategy where we observe the value of the underlying at time t, denoted xptq. If
xptq ¡ xpt  1q, then we assume that the value of the underlying at time t   1 will be higher than the one at time
t. Whenever the prediction is rising, we buy at most ∆shares of stocks. Otherwise, we sell all stocks we have. This
model performs well in a trending market, but losses a lot in a mean-reverting market.
2.4.5.5
The measures
The measures used when forecasting ﬁnancial time series with machine learning models can
roughly be divided into three main groups: Error-based, Return-based, and Accuracy-based. Here, error is used when
forecasting market prices or returns, while accuracy is used in a directional sense. That is, correctness corresponds to
the forecasted variable actually moving in the same direction as the forecast. The most popular measures are accuracy,
annual return, and root mean squared error. Note, accuracy does not convey any information on proﬁtability, and
simulations must be performed. Thus, we should favour performance metrics demonstrating the return capabilities of
an algorithm with respect to its ﬁeld of forecasting or classiﬁcation.
Linear measures are measurements in one direction. For example length, width, or height of objects, and distance
between two points are linear measures. Linearity also also express the idea that the model possesses the property of
additivity and homogeneity, meaning that a change in one variable causes a proportional change in another variable.
An example of a linear measure is the root-mean-square deviation (RMSD), or, root-mean-square error (RMSE), which
is used to measure the differences between values predicted by a model or an estimator and the values observed.
In mathematics and science, a nonlinear system is a system in which the change of the output is not proportional to the
change of the input. Systems can be deﬁned as nonlinear, regardless of whether known linear functions appear in the
equations. Nonlinearity measures are a means of quantifying the size of nonlinearity in the Input/Output-behaviour of
nonlinear systems (more than one dimension). Methods that efﬁciently compute nonlinearity measures are based on
convex optimisation.
Note, linear measures do not account for the non-linearity of RNN models (see Bellgard et al.
[1999]). One way
around is to assess the performance of a model using trading simulation.
2.4.5.6
Analysing models
Meta-analysis is the statistical analysis of a large collection of results from individual
studies for the purpose of integrating the ﬁndings. In ﬁnance, the lack of standardised testing metrics and standard
testing datasets makes it difﬁcult to form a meaningful metaregression analysis. One solution is to evaluate algorithm
19
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
classes based on their relative rank in subgroup experiments. Ryll et al. [2019] proposed an average-over-all approach,
where for a given algorithm type, a single rank score between 0 and 1 is computed. They argued that pairwise rank
analysis based on the same scoring system uncovers meaningful performance differences between algorithm classes.
2.5
Using neural networks to model returns
2.5.1
An overview
Among the various techniques for classifying and forecasting ﬁnancial time series, fundamental and technical analysis are the most popular ones (see Hu et al.
[2015]) (see Appendix (11)). Even though statistical procedures are
widely used for patterns recognition, the effectiveness of these methods relies both on model’s assumptions and prior
knowledge on data properties. To remedy these pitfalls, several classiﬁers developed, using various data mining and
computational intelligence methods such as rule induction, fuzzy rule induction, decision trees, neural networks etc.
In 1990, Kimoto et al. [1990] applied a modular neural network machine learning algorithm to predict the movement
of stock index of Tokyo Stock Exchange. Schoneburg [1990] used neural networks to predict stock prices. BaBa
et al.
[1992] used neural networks to build an intelligent forecasting system of stock prices. Cheng [1994] used
neural network for forecasting the price-volume relationship in the Taiwan stock market. Tsibouris et al.
[1995]
tested the efﬁcient markets hypothesis with gradient descent algorithms. Since ANN developed further, it has been
widely applied to stock analysis. Kuo [1998] considered fuzzy neural networks for building a decision support system
for predicting the stock market. Nowadays, the best recognised tools in the currency markets is the artiﬁcial neural
networks (ANNs), supported by numerous empirical studies (see Ahmed et al.
[2010]). Similarly, Krollner et al.
[2010] provided a survey on forecasting ﬁnancial time series with machine learning and found that ANNs were the
dominant technique in that ﬁeld. The foremost reason for using ANNs is that there is some nonlinear aspect to the
forecasting problem under consideration, taking the form of a complex nonlinear relationship between the independent and dependent variables. The characteristics of ﬁnancial time series, such as equity stock or currency markets,
are inﬂuenced by the psychology of traders (behavioural ﬁnance) and are strongly non-linear and hardly predictable
(see Maknickiene et al. [2011]).
One advantage in using an ANNs is that the researcher does not need to know a priori the type of functional relationship
existing between the independent and dependent variables (see Darbellay et al. [2000]). A vast literature demonstrated
that neural networks perform better than conventional statistic approaches in ﬁnancial forecasting (see Refenes et al.
[1994], Adya et al.
[1998], Abu-Mostafa et al.
[2001]). For many ﬁnancial forecasting problems, classiﬁcation
models work better than point prediction. Further, Qi [2001] argued that due to the continually changing nature
of ﬁnancial relationships, ANNs are more likely to outperform traditional techniques when the input data is kept
as current as possible. This is done by recursive modeling, where the researcher adds new observations and drops
the oldest ones each time a new time series forecast is made (sliding window). Olson et al.
[2003] compared
neural network forecasts of one-year ahead Canadian stock returns with the forecasts obtained by using ordinary least
squares (OLS) and logistic regression techniques and showed that the backpropagation algorithm outperformed the
best regression alternatives for both point estimation and in classifying ﬁrms expected to have either high or low
returns This superiority of the NNs translated into greater proﬁtability using various trading rules. Using data from
four major stock market indexes, Fok et al. [2008] compared linear regression and neural network backpropagation
by testing their forecasting performances. They showed that the latter had better prediction accuracy. Ballings et
al. [2015] compared ensemble methods such as Random Forest, AdaBoost, Kernel Factory, against single classiﬁer
models such as NNs, logistic regression, SVM, and K-nearest neighbour using data from 5767 European companies.
The performance measures were a ﬁve time two-fold cross-validation and area under the curve (AUC) to predict long
term stock price direction and reported the Random Forest as the best algorithm.
Lin et al.
[2009] used Echo State Network to forecast the next closing price. Huang et al.
[2009] combined
support vector regression (SVR) with a self-organising feature map (SOFM) and feature selection based on ﬁltering
for predicting the next day price of the Taiwan index futures. Lee [2009] developed a prediction model on the basis
20
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
of an SVM with a hybrid feature selection method to ﬁnd the best input features. The F-score was used as a selection
criterion. Cogo [2015] considered several ANN-based variable selection models for predicting one-day ahead price
of the S&P 500 index. The architecture is a three layer network with ten neurons in the hidden layer. Three algorithms
are tested for training the networks on one hundred days. The best results are obtained with the Levenberg-Marquardt
algorithm where the predicted price mimic fairly well the patterns of the actual one for the ﬁrst ﬁfty days. The ANN
trained with gradient descent training algorithm exhibited poor performances. The author also investigated the impact
of the number of neurons in the hidden layer in the prediction process. The sizes tested were 20, 50, and 150 neurons
and the author concluded that with one hidden layer the increased number of neurons resulted in lower predictive
power. Liu et al.
[2016] investigated logistic regression, Gaussian discriminant analysis (GDA), Naive Bayes, and
SVM to forecast the movement of the S&P 500 index. After optimising the features selection for different models, the
SVM model with a Radial Basis Function (RBF) kernel obtained a rate of accuracy of 62.5%. for future market trend.
Zhong et al. [2017a] used deep ANN to forecast daily stock market return using dimensionality reduction. Bao et al.
[2017] considered deep nonlinear NN topologies to predict ﬁnancial time series. Di Persio et al. [2017] compared
a basic multi-layer RNN, LSTM, and GRU on forecasting Google stock price movements (difference in price) on
different time horizons: the next day, the next 5 days, the 10 days. They found LSTM to be the better algorithm. The
accuracy for next day is about 66%, and for 5 days horizon it is about 72%. Yang et al. [2017] proposed an ensemble
of multi-layer feedforward networks to predict the Chinese stock. Three networks are trained with backpropagation
and combined with the bagging approach (see Appendix (13.2)). Zhang et al. [2018] proposed a stock price trend
prediction system to predict both the price movement and its interval of growth (or decline) rate within predeﬁned
prediction intervals. A Random Forest was trained to classify clips of stocks into four classes (up, down, ﬂat, and
unknown) according to the shapes of their close prices. They outperformed existing prediction methods in terms of
accuracy and return per trade. Abbas [2018] used a deep learning model (with four hidden layers) to predict stock
prices (returns) for major stock indices. He considered short term prediction with a frequency of the minute. The data
consisted of over 40, 000 minutes of S&P 500 index. The idea was to ﬁnd emerging trends in minute. The training
was done by batches and a fully conducted batch is an epoch. After ten epochs, the MSE on the training set was 0.038.
2.5.2
Combining neural networks with evolutionary algorithms
Some of the disadvantages of NNs are long training time, unwanted convergence to local instead of global optimal
solution, and large number of parameters (see Lee et al. [1991]). One way forward is to combine ANN with another
algorithm such as evolutionary computing tools that can take care of a speciﬁc problem (see Appendix (20)). Many
research papers have appeared in the literature using evolutionary computing tools, such as genetic algorithm (GA)
(see Montana et al.
[1989], Nair et al.
[2011], Contras et al.
[2016]), particle swarm optimization (PSO) (see
Kennedy et al. [1995] Jordehi et al. [2013]), bacterial foraging optimization (BFO) (see Jhankal et al. [2011]), and
Adaptive bacterial foraging optimization (ABFO) in developing forecasting models.
Inspired by the pattern exhibited by bacterial foraging behaviour, Helstrom et al.
[1998] proposed an evolutionary
computation technique called Bacterial foraging optimization (BFO). It analyses how the run-length unit parameter
of BFO controls the exploration of the whole search space and the exploitation of the promising areas. Shin et al.
[1998] proposed data mining approach using genetic algorithms (GA) to solve the knowledge acquisition problems
that are inherent in constructing and maintaining rule-based applications for stock market. Kim et al.
[2000] used
GA to improve the learning algorithm, and also to reduce the complexity of the feature space. Kim et al.
[2004b]
developed a feature transformation method using genetic algorithms. It reduces the dimensionality of the feature
space and removes irrelevant factors involved in stock price prediction. Jamous et al.
[2016] considered particle
swarm optimization with center mass (PSOCOM) technique to develop an efﬁcient forecasting model. Rani et al.
[2017] presented an overview on optimisation techniques and hybridisation to improve ANN performance (see details
in Appendix (20)).
