2.5.3
Study
Bahrammirzaee [2010] presented a study of applications of computational intelligence to ﬁnancial data and concluded
that artiﬁcial intelligence algorithms generally possess a higher accuracy than comparable statistical methods. Similarly, Cavalcante et al.
[2016] presented a study of applications of computational intelligence to ﬁnancial data in
studies from 2009-2015. Makridakis et al.
[2018] evaluated the forecasting performance of ML against statistical
models across multiple forecasting horizons using a large subset of 1045 monthly time series used in the M3 Competition. Statistical models were found to be better across both accuracy measures used and for all forecasting horizons
examined. Shah et al. [2019] presented a review of methods for predicting stock prices. Ryll et al. [2019] studied
over 150 related articles on applying machine learning to ﬁnancial market forecasting. They classiﬁed the different algorithms and conducted rank analyses to assess the comparative performance of the different algorithm classes. They
found signiﬁcant evidence of the outperformance of machine learning against traditional stochastic models. Further,
on average, recurrent neural networks outperform feed forward neural networks as well as support vector machines,
implying the existence of exploitable temporal dependencies in ﬁnancial time series across multiple asset classes and
geographies.
2.5.4
Generative networks
Zhou et al.
[2018] proposed a GAN model (see Appendix (19)) using long short-term memory (LSTM) and a
convolutional neural network (CNN) for adversarial training to forecast high-frequency stock market. The method
of rolling partition training and testing set is used to analyse the effect of the model update cycle on the prediction
performance. Basic technical index data was used as input variable. They improved the prediction accuracy of the
price direction and reduced forecast error between real prices and forecast ones.
Zhang et al.
[2019] proposed a GAN model with the multi-layer perceptron (MLP) as the discriminator D and
the long short-term memory (LSTM) as the generator G for forecasting the closing price of stocks. The latter mine
the data distributions of stocks from given data in the stock market and generate data with the same distributions,
while the former aims to discriminate the real stock data and generated data. The daily S&P 500 index was tested to
predict the next day closing price. Twenty years of daily data together with 7 ﬁnancial factors (high, low, open, close
price, volume, turnover rate and Ma5) were used to predict the future closing price. These factors (technical analysis)
occurred to be very valuable and signiﬁcant in the forecasting process. Several statistical indicators of performance
such as MAE, RMSE, MAPE were chosen, and the results were found to be competitive compared with other methods.
In fact they were very comparable to those obtained with LSTM, slightly lower.
Koshiyama et al.
[2019] used a conditional GAN (cGAN) model for trading strategies calibration (or ﬁne-tuning)
and aggregation (portfolio allocation). The data set was made of 579 assets (mainly equity). The model was tested
against an ensemble scheme and model validation methods, and the cGAN model was found to outperform the baseline
models.
2.5.5
Towards reservoir computing
Miller [2018] argued that DNN models, such as autoregressive Wavenet and Transformer, are replacing RNNs on a
diverse set of tasks. This is based on several studies (see Bai et al. [2018]) showing that feedforward networks could
match the results of the best recurrent models on benchmark sequence tasks such as language modelling, machine
translation, music generation. Miller et al. [2019] proved that stable recurrent neural networks (meaning the gradients
can not explode) were well approximated by feedforward networks for the purpose of both inference and training by
gradient descent. However, feedforward networks make a strong conditional independence assumption. This is not the
case of market returns which are multifractal. Further, the origin of this discussion derived from the vanishing and the
exploding gradient problems when training recurrent neural networks (see Appendix (16.4.3)). The problem disappear
when considering Reservoir Computing (RC). Thus, it would make sense to use RC when forecasting market returns.
