Quantitative Analytics
2.5.6
Reinforcement learning for predicting patterns
Since ﬁnancial patterns are distinctive formations of market prices, forecasting them is a multi-step prediction-learning
problem (see Appendix (16.1)). However, as discussed above, research focussed on predicting market prices (regresion
problem) and their directions (classiﬁcation problem) as it it was a single-step prediction-learning problem by using
supervised learning methods. Sutton [1988] showed that temporal difference (TD) methods were more efﬁcient than
supervised learning methods when the data sequences were generated by a dynamical system. In a multi-step problem,
we are concerned with experience coming in observation-outcome sequences of the form x1, x2, ..., xm, z, where each
xt is a vector of observable input at time t in the sequence, and z P R is the outcome of that sequence. In supervised
learning, each sequence of observations and its outcome is represented by a sequence of observation-outcome pair of
the form pxt, zq at time t. As such, we can not capture the transition probabilities of the dynamical system driving
the successive relative changes in market prices. TD algorithms are at the heart of reinforcement learning, solving the
prediction problem, but they do not provide an optimal policy. It uses the evaluation step in the learning process, and
not the improvement step.
3
Forecasting returns
It is now well known that market returns are multifractal, exhibiting trends that repeat over time, and some, following
sinusoidal shape (see Lo et al. [2002]). As a result, future prices and trends can be predicted based on past prices.
Thus, price prediction is closely related to time series analysis and trend modelling. Some examples include stochastic
process theory, spectral analysis, wavelet analysis, among others. Some popular stochastic models in ﬁnance are the
autoregressive integrated moving average (ARIMA) and the generalised autoregressive conditional heteroscedasticity
(GARCH) (see Box et al. [1976]). With the increasing volume of data stored, soft computing methods developed, such
as fuzzy logic and neural networks (NNs). Gately [1995] introduced ANN to the ﬁnancial industry. Logically, hybrid
models between stochastic models and networks developed. Zhang [2003] proposed to combine ARIMA and ANN
to model time series of market prices. The results outperformed both models separately. This model was improved by
Khashei et al. [2010]. New and different hybrid models developed. For instance, Guresen et al. [2011] combined
a GARCH and EGARCH model with different ANN structures. Adding to purely statistical analysis, some authors
incorporated risk analysis by considering technical analysis (TA) (see Appendix (11)). For instance, Witkowska et al.
[2005] combined TA with ANN models to forecast the WIG20 index on the Warsaw Stock Exchange. One can also
combine TA and fundamental analyses with ANN for forecasting stock prices (see Faustryjak et al. [2018]). Paluch
et al. [2014] proposed to combine technical indicators with fractal indicators. See Appendix (11.4) for a description
of fractal indicators. Then, Paluch et al. [2015] [2016] [2018a] considered hybrid ANN-based models with technical
and fractal analysis for forecasting closing prices on the Warsaw stock exchange (WSE). The system analysed prices
trends and chosed assets that will achieve the highest expected proﬁt the next day. The system was tested ofﬂine on
historical data from 70 companies over a period of six months. The prediction was correct 60% of the time, and a
proﬁt of 17.2% was generated, while the WIG20 index made 0.99% and the mWIG40 made 5.69%. Paluch et al.
[2018b] proposed a hybrid model combining technical and fractal analysis with ANN to predict close prices in the
WSE. The system comprised new fractal analysis indicators.
We are brieﬂy going to present the hybrid method proposed by Paluch et al.
[2018b] as an example of an ANN
model to forecast closing prices. This is because ANNs are not designed to forecast serially correlated time series.
As discussed in Section (2.5), recurrent neural networks outperform traditional artiﬁcial neural networks as well as
support vector machines, implying the existence of exploitable temporal dependencies in ﬁnancial time series across
multiple asset classes and geographies. Hence, we are going to focus on RNN to forecast market returns.
In order to gain some insight on the capability of neural networks to reproduce the dynamics of ﬁnancial time series,
we need to test some of the properties of recurrent neural networks introduced in Section (2.5) as well as some of their
characteristic for modelling changing environment presented in Section (2.2.2). Following Section (2.4), we will test
neural networks on the Mackey-Glass signal as well as several real-time ﬁnancial time series.
Among the different ML models presented in Section (2.2), we are going to concentrate on random projection networks
23
