Quantitative Analytics
(RPN) with non-linear spatio-temporal projections generating transient and attractor dynamics (see Wojcik [2018],
Andras [2018]). We will focus on the Associative Reservoir Computing (ARC) (see Reinhart et al. [2008] [2009b])
as it is stable and fast, and, uniﬁes the RPN models. The detailed implementation of the model is given in Appendix
(17.1). The model will be tested both with ofﬂine and online algorithms. We will also test a method for teaching the
reservoir how to remain consistently stable while feeding it with noisy input.
3.1
Neural models for price forecasting
Paluch et al.
[2018b] proposed a hybrid model combining technical analysis (TA) and fractal analysis (FA) (see
Appendix (11)) with ANN to forecast the closing price of an asset for the next day. The structure of the model is
displayed in Figure (5). In the ﬁrst module (in blue), the TA and FA indicators are ﬁrst computed. They are then used
as input in the ANN, which output the closing price for the next day (time t  k   1). Feed-forward ANNs of the
MLP type were considered. Three kind of hybrid models were tested: TA with ANN, FA with ANN, and TA plus FA
combined with ANN.
Figure 5: Structure of hybrid model
As discussed in Section (4), technical indicators (TI) give trend indications and are used for forecasting the directions
of the underlying asset, and not the next day closing prices. Hence, the authors introduced a moving time window
method based on current and past close market prices, which was applied to all three hybrid models. These prices are
entered as input to the ANN. It corresponds to the closed switch S in Figure (5).
Different two-layer MLP structures were tested with different number of inputs and neurons in the hidden layer. A
combination of input data was tested. The MSE was used as criterion for selection of the best model. The benchmark
model was a purely ANN-based model with the moving time window method (past close prices). Data consisted of
historical data for ﬁve companies listed on the Warsaw Stock Exchange. The structures with closed switch S (past
close prices) produced the best results. Hybrid models performed better than the benchmark. The FA with ANN and
TA plus FA with ANN were marginally better than TA with ANN, and generated very similar results.
When forecasting market returns it is more judicious to consider a recurrent neural network rather than a neural
network. The latter will be discussed when forecasting the directions of the underlying asset.
3.2
The associative reservoir computing
Using a particular impementation of the ARC (see Appendix (17.1)), we reproduce below some results on the MackeyGlass signal and FX time series obtained by a student of ours who did his MSc thesis at QFL (see Bouizi [2017]).
3.2.1
Using Ofﬂine algorithms
3.2.1.1
The Mackey-Glass series
In the academic literature, it is found that pattern generation can be easily implemented and produces very good results. However, the Mackey-Glass series prediction task is more complex and
