good. One solution could be to ﬁnd appropriate ﬁlter thresholds to correlate them artiﬁcially on the validation sample
and see if the correlation is maintained on the testing sample. It is important to point out that there is optimisation to
be done for this issue as a too high or too low threshold would both lead to high correlation but poor performance.
However, there may exist some thresholds leading to good learning and good enough correlation.
4
Forecasting directions
We are going to describe an algorithm for forecasting the directions of the underlying asset. We will then discuss
adaptive decision trading systems that can be applied to all stocks.
33
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
4.1
Introduction
4.1.1
An overview
As discussed in Section (3), the prediction of ﬁnancial time series is a highly challenging task since they are multifractal, thus exhibiting non-Gaussian distribution, the presence of extreme values, and long-range dependent dynamics. It
requires models to capture subtle functional relationships among the empirical data even though the underlying relationships are unknown or hard to describe. Fortunately, such relationships are not required as far as predicting future
trend is concerned. This is because the direction of an underlying asset refers to the movement of the asset price or the
trend of ﬂuctuation in the underlying asset in the future. Therefore, the change of a feature over time is more important
than the absolute value of a feature. Further, forecasting the trends results in networks that are less complex than when
forecasting the price levels. Precise forecasting of the trends of the underlying asset can be extremely advantageous
for investors.
Machine learning (ML) models, such as support vector machine (SVM) and reinforcement learning, have been extensively studied when forecasting the market price directions (see Moody et al.
[2001], Huang et al.
[2005]). Kim
[2003] used SVM to predict the direction of the daily stock price movements on the Korean market, obtaining a hit
rate of 56%. Academics also examined Random Forests, K-nearest neighbour, and Gaussian naive Bayes before considering to more complex models such as ANNs and Deep NNs. In an empirical study, Gerlein et al. [2016] showed
that models such as C4.5, K, logistic model tree, were in the range of 48%  54%. However, the performance of
a stock market predictor heavily depends on the correlation between the data used for training and the current input
(feature) for prediction.
Several studies explored the relationship between some initial input variables (also called factors or predictors) and
the closing price of the considered underlying asset. The features are based on fundamental and technical factors. For
instance, Kaastra et al. [1996] analysed the importance of knowing the input variables when forecasting ﬁnancial time
series. It is generally assumed that the input variables have a predictive power. However, the forecasting power of
networks is quite sensitive to the capacity of identifying the right input variables (features, other correlated underlying
assets, technical indicators) that will best explain the future variations of the underlying asset. Given the increasing
processing power of computers, academics are now trying all kind of explanatory variables, combining them in a very
large input set, hoping that ML will identify some relationships with the process to be forecasted. Further, some textmining based methods developed to automatically analyse fundamental factors such as ﬁnancial status, development
strategies, economic conditions psychology of investors (see Ruiz et al.
[2012], DingEtAl15). Schumaker et al.
[2009] used text-mining technique to forecast, obtaining a hit rate of 57%.
The paradigm has been shifted from identifying the best statistical model to identifying the best input variables,
considering the ML model as a black box which will always learn the relationship Y  fpXq between the input and
output. The motto is to let the data speak for themselves. In addition, when the forecasting results are not satisfactory,
the data scientists will occasionally change the black box fpq and repeat the tests. There is no-longer a need to analyse
the data and scientiﬁcally propose a predictive model capable of accounting for the properties of the data at hand. One
wonder where the race will end.
4.1.2
The input variables
4.1.2.1
Fundamental factors
In the early days, the features selected for the inputs of the networks were mostly
derived from the data within the same market under concern, leaving out important information. One solution is to
consider sentiment analysis, which infer the attitudes of several key ﬁgures or successful analysts in the markets, and
then interpolate the general sentiment of investors. However, this approach is strongly biased towards people opinions.
Another solution is to consider the use of global stock data as the input features to the networks. Since markets are
interconnected, Shen et al. [2012] proposed to consider spatial correlation and combine stock markets, commodity
prices and foreign currency data as potential features. Further, one can also consider the temporal correlation between
34
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
the closing prices of the markets that stop trading right before the considered market, or at the beginning of that
market. In the latter, we can calculate the autocorrelation and cross-correlation of different market trends as a measure
for selecting input features.
We can also consider the correlation of market trends in longer term. In general, prediction accuracy increases when
time span becomes longer.
4.1.2.2
Technical indicators
Over the last twenty years, traders and academics have been using technical analysis
as well as certain fundamental valuation metrics to improve results when forecasting trends (see Gencay [1997],
Metghalchi et al.
[2007]). The theoretical basis of technical indicators (TIs) is the concept of momentum, where
a momentum oscillator is used to measure the velocity or rate of change of price over time (see Appendix (11.2)).
Further, some economists and statisticians showed that technical indicators have predictive power and found a good
measure of support for their effectiveness (see Chen et al. [2009], Metghalchi et al. [2012] [2015]). These properties
led some data scientists to incorporate such trading strategies in machine learning in order to forecast future price
directions (see Tanaka-Yamawaki et al. [2007], Lee et al. [2007], Senol et al. [2008], Kara et al. [2011], Patel et al.
[2015], Qiu et al. [2016]).
4.1.3
Selection and representation
4.1.3.1
Selecting the technical indicators
Given the large number of technical indicators, some practitioners proposed to use a speciﬁc model for selecting the best TIs to enter the network. Kim et al. [2000] used neural network
modiﬁed by genetic algorithm (GA). Kwong [2004] used GA to get a dynamic mechanism for selecting appropriate factors from fundamental data and TIs. Mitra [2009] combined signals generated from moving averages of
different window size using ANN models. Huang et al.
[2009] combined support vector regression (SVR) with a
self-organising feature map (SOFM) and feature selection based on ﬁltering for predicting the next day price of the
Taiwan index futures. Lee [2009] developed a prediction model on the basis of an SVM with a hybrid feature selection
method to ﬁnd the best input features. Chavan et al. [2013] surveyed different input parameters that could be used
for stock market prediction with ANN and concluded that hybridised parameters like combination of technical and
fundamental variables gave better prediction accuracy. Adebiyi et al. [2012] also combined the technical and fundamental variables using ANN models. Chandwani et al. [2014] combined both technical and fundamental parameters
to forecast price directions on the Indian stock market. They integrated genetic algorithm (GA) with two classiﬁcation
techniques, the ANN and SVM, where GA is used to select the set of the most informative input features from among
all the technical and ﬁnancial indicators. They showed that GA signiﬁcantly increased the accuracy of ANN.
However, forecasting trends inherently perform better in trending markets (bull or bear markets) than in markets that
might display volatility and be trendless over the examination period.
4.1.3.2
Representing the technical indicators
There are two main approaches when using technical indicators:
1. one can either directly use the continuous technical indicators (actual time series), or
2. one can choose to represent them as trend deterministic data (discrete in nature) (see Appendix (11.5.3)).
4.2
The predictive algorithm
We are going to present some methods for forecasting the underlying directions. We will also show that we need
a feature selection algorithm to select the features that contribute most to the accuracy of prediction using different
machine learning algorithms. The direction measures used to assess the predictive power of the algorithm are deﬁned
in Appendix (9.4.2).
35
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
4.2.1
Selecting the input variables
When designing neural networks we need to select the most relevant input variables with best forecasting power to
reduce the number of inputs used to train the networks and improve accuracy. Since both input and output variables are
noisy, variable selection models developed that provide appropriate inputs by a trial and error process (see Deboeck
[1994]). They decide on the selection of the inputs to be used and their re-sampling frequency. For instance, sensitivity
analysis is used to determine the importance of the inputs to the neural network. It consists in ranking each input based
on the contribution to the neural network and eliminating the bottom of the list. That is, one selects a set of data as
input, train a network until an acceptable level of performance is achieved, apply sensitivity analysis and eliminate
all the variables below a predeﬁned threshold. Alternatively, one can determine the impact that setting an input to its
average value has on the overall performance of the ANN. If the network is not using an input to discriminate between
possible outcomes, ﬁxing that input to its average value should actually improve the overall performance.
4.2.2
Representing the input variables
There are two main approaches when using technical indicators. When selecting the input variables of the predictive
model, we can:
1. Directly use continuous technical indicators (actual time series): Kara et al.
[2011] proposed a method in
the case where the inputs are the continuous technical indicators. The values of all technical indicators are
normalised in the range between r1,  1s so that larger value of one indicator do not overwhelm the smaller
valued indicator. Given a (one hidden layer) neural network (NN), the output can be a single neuron with a
sigmoid transfer function, which results in a continuous value output between 0 and 1 (see Figure ( 16)). A
threshold of 1
2 is used to determine the up and down direction prediction, where the former occurs for a value
greater than 1
2 and the latter occurs for a value smaller than 1
2.
2. Choose to represent them as trend deterministic data (discrete in nature): In the case where we choose to
represent the indicators as trend deterministic data (TDD), Patel et al. [2015] proposed the Trend Deterministic
Data Preparation Layer (TDDPL) (see Figure ( 17)). It extracts trend related information from each of the
technical indicators by converting continuous-valued technical parameters to discrete value (up  1, down 1),
representing the trend (see details in Appendix (11.5.3)). The idea being that if a prediction model is given
direct continuous-valued input, it will try to establish relation between the values at different time steps, which
is not required as far as predicting future trend is considered. What matter is how its value (up  1, down 1)
has changed with respect to previous time step rather than the absolute value of change. Each input parameters
in its discrete form indicates a possible up or down trend determined based on its inherent property. Thus, when
using TDDPL, we are directly inputting the trend based such that prediction models only have to determine
co-relation between the input trends and output trend (which is easier than determining relation within serially
correlated continuous time series).
