where 1 indicates a positive return and 0 a negative return.
The machine learning (ML) model is trained to recognise valuable signals from invaluable ones so that whenever the
predictive probability on a positive return exceeds a cutoff value the trade is executed. The overall return generated by
the model is then calculated by adding up the returns of the performed trades.
4.3
A hybrid algorithm
The mathematical foundations of modern statistics were laid in the 17th century with the development of the probability theory by Cardano, Pascal and de Fermat. The modern ﬁeld of statistics emerged in the late 19th and early
20th century, where Galton and Karl Pearson transformed statistics into a rigorous mathematical discipline used for
analysis. It was further developed by Fisher in the 1920s and reﬁned by Egon Pearson and Neyman in the 1930s.
Since that time, statistics is a mathematical body of science that pertains to the collection, analysis, interpretation or
explanation, and presentation of data. It is concerned with the use of data in the context of uncertainty and decision
making in the face of uncertainty. It therefore makes sense to combine statistics with machine learning.
Since machine learning algorithms do not require any assumptions about the data, hybrid algorithms developed combining big data analytic techniques with machine learning algorithms. Multivariate analysis consider dimensionality
reduction technologies, such as principal component analysis (PCA), fuzzy robust principal component analysis (FRPCA) and kernel-based principal component analysis (KPCA), among others. Zhong et al. [2017a] presented a study
of dimensionality reduction with an application to predict the daily return direction of the SPDR S&P 500 ETF (ticker
symbol: SPY) using ANN classiﬁers. They showed that PCA-based ANN classiﬁers produced the best predictor of
the ETF daily return direction over various datasets transformed using PCA. Zhong et al. [2017b] performed a comprehensive data mining procedure, including both cluster and classiﬁcation mining, to forecast the ETF daily return
direction. They showed that PCA-based ANN classiﬁers lead to signiﬁcantly higher accuracy than three different
PCA-based logistic regression models.
Zhong et al. [2019] proposed a classiﬁcation mining procedure based on hybrid machine learning algorithms. They
studied deep neural networks (DNNs) and traditional artiﬁcial neural networks (ANNs), acting as classiﬁers, in the
task of predicting daily return directions of the SPDR S&P 500 ETF based on 60 ﬁnancial and economic features
taken as input. The models were tested on untransformed dataset, along with two datasets transformed via principal
38
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
component analysis (PCA). The principal components can be written as the linear combinations of all the factors with
the corresponding coefﬁcients equalling the elements of the eigenvectors (see Appendix (10.1.4)).
Network with a large number of hidden layers have been developed to extract the classiﬁcation rules from the trained
neural networks. For instance, the multilayer feed-forward network is most commonly used for pattern recognition,
including classiﬁcation, in data mining. In their terminology, ANNS have up to ten layers, while DNNs have more than
ten layers. The authors considered multilayer feed-forward neural networks speciﬁcally designed for classiﬁcation
problems (see Chiang et al. [2016], Kim et al. [2016]). These networks are used for pattern recognition, including
classiﬁcation, in data mining.
The data is preprocessed to account for missing values, outliers, and it is normalised. The data is then transformed
using PCA. Once the models have been trained, the aim is to recognise the different patterns of new data during the
testing stage. Here, direction forecast (up or down) are more likely to be on the right side of the trade, leading to
forecasting models with high classiﬁcation accuracy. The difference between the predicted class and the true class
is measured by the mean squared error (MSE). Successful direction forecasts have a prediction accuracy closer to
60%. The model is best trained in the sense that the validation phase achieves its lowest MSE with the trained
model. The simulation results showed that the DNNs using two PCA-represented datasets produce signiﬁcantly higher
classiﬁcation accuracy than those using the entire untransformed dataset.
