4.5
Adaptive decision trading systems
Trading systems are often highly dependent on preselected inputs and model parameters that are expected to continue
providing trading information well after the initial training or back-tested model development period. However, given
the endlessly changing futures market (multifractal market), one must consider a decision support systems (DSS), or
rule change trading system (RCTS), based on numerous trading rules generated by some statistical and probability
analysis to cover diverse market conditions.
Some authors tried to ﬁnd one model that could be applied to all stocks. That is, describe the relationship among stock
index variables and determine the best representative variables and best theory models for stock indices. They must
optimise system performance based on a speciﬁc timeframe and desired output. For instance, Samaras et al. [2004]
40
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
proposed a multi-criteria decision support system that aims at presenting an evaluation of stocks on the Athens Stock
Exchange on the basis of fundamental analysis. Chavarnakul et al.
[2009] used technical analysis (see Appendix
(11)), along with employing a neural network as a decision maker, for stock trading. Chang et al. [2009] developed
a three-stage system for stock trading prediction, called CBDWNN. These stages are: (1) screening out the potential
stocks and the important inﬂuential factors; (2) using a backpropagation neural network to predict the buy/sell points
of stock price; and (3) adopting case-based dynamic windows to further improve the forecasting results.
In general, a model ﬁtting one security well may poorly perform another one. We must therefore consider an adaptive
decision trading system (ADTS). For instance, Chiang et al.
[2016] proposed a system that adapts both the inputs
and the prediction model based on the desired output. This is an adaptive decision support system model, where
multiple inputs and modelling techniques are utilised. It adaptively creates a new prediction model for every single
security. Potential variables facilitating the best network performance are selected and used in the model as inputs. For
instance, the closing price and volume of the stock index for the last trading day together with technical measurements
are considered. The output layer has one node, which is the stock movement direction. As discussed above, the output
from the neural network is standardised between zero and one and split in two, leading to three scenarios: buy, hold,
and sell. Given that with N potential variables, the different possible combination of inputs for the neural network
will reach 2N  1, the authors decreased the size of the neural network used to ﬁve variables, hence ﬁve inputs. In that
setting, the number of combination for inputs is
C 
