where Cn
r
1 is the number of combination of r objects taken from n different objects. There are four basic modules:
the data pre-processing module, the neural network initialisation module, the neural networking training module, and
the output module. The learning algorithm minimise the mean square error (MSE) between the estimated and real
output. Direction prediction correctness (DPC) is obtained by calculating the percentage of correct predictions during
the testing period. In order to assess the potential beneﬁt of the model, simulated returns following the trading signal
output are produced which are compared against a buy-and-hold strategy. Note, higher prediction accuracy does not
always guarantee higher return. Thus, forecasting the direction alone is not sufﬁcient and must be combined with
some criteria on the generated returns when determining the ﬁnal predictive model. During real market transactions,
the model with the highest return is used as the ﬁnal adoption model for the next prediction. Since daily movements
are usually small, carrying more noise, direction predictions near 55% are sufﬁcient to generate proﬁt. The system
provided signiﬁcantly higher return than the buy-and-hold strategy.
As discussed above, since neural networks have difﬁculty catching-up with the small changes, one should consider
denoising the data with PCA or wavelet transformation before providing it to the network. The resulting trends of
these data are clearer. Using the same system on this smoothed data, one can get higher returns. The system can
ignores small ﬂuctuations and remains effective in detecting larger trends, reducing the transaction fees.
In practice, trading rules are difﬁcult to apply in the future due to endless changes in the market due to its multifractal
nature. To account for changing market conditions, Kim et al. [2016] proposed a rule change trading system (RCTS)
based on previous trading results. The system uses rough set analysis to extract a number of trading rules in order
to cover diverse market situations and then change the trading rules according to trading performance. Rough set
theory (applies to discrete data) is used for stock market analysis, market timing, and trading rule generation. Many
reduction methods exist to discretise the data, such as manual reducers, genetic algorithms, the Johnson algorithm,
and the dynamic reduct method. To facilitate the rule change mechanism, a genetic algorithm is employed with the
objective function of maximising the payoff ratio to determine the thresholds of market timing for both buying and
selling in the futures market.
