5
Forecasting returns and directions
We are going to describe an algorithm for forecasting the both the price level and the directions of the underlying asset.
At last, we will draw conclusions on the results and deﬁne a set of rules we want to follow when building our trading
algorithm.
5.1
Improving the algorithm
5.1.1
Discussion
We have seen in Section (4.5) that accuracy could be a good measure for the general ability of a network, but that
it does not convey any information on proﬁtability. Thus, we should favour return metrics taking into account the
magnitude of the trends that a system discovers (see Kim et al. [2016]). For instance, when forecasting directions,
the data should be denoised and the trading decisions should be combined with some criteria on the generated returns.
As a result, the model with the highest return should be selected as the ﬁnal adoption model for the next prediction.
However, while this is a good choice, nothing guarantees that the past will repeat itself. One solution is to consider a
model that also forecast the price level for the next period.
We are now going to describe a framework for simultaneously forecasting both future price levels and asset price
directions by considering an optimum combination of
• technical indicators (see Appendix (11.2)),
• trading rules, and
• trading strategies (see Appendix (11.5)).
To do so, we will combine the predictive model proposed by Van den Poel et al. [2016] with the proposed forecasting
model presented in Section (3), which we will enlarge by incorporating the trading strategies proposed by Metghalchi
et al. [2018].
5.1.2
Description
We assume that we can denoise the data with PCA, wavelet decomposition, or other ﬁltering tools.
5.1.2.1
Selection layer
We are now going to explain how to enlarge the algorithm presented in Section (4.2) by
incorporating the trading strategies proposed by Metghalchi et al.
[2018]. At each time step we will select the
most efﬁcient trading strategy among the list of strategy given in Appendix (11.5.2). To do so, we propose the Meta
Buy/Sell Signal (MBSS) inferred from all the predictors generated by the selection layer. We are going to build a level
of conﬁdence around the MBSS and use it to select the most efﬁcient strategy. As discussed above, at a given time
t, the inputs of the latter are all the independent variables and the output is the direction triplet p1, 1, 0q for each
independent variable. In order to get a meta buy/sell decision signal, we choose to combine all the direction triplets
with a majority voting rule (or any other rule) (see Appendix (11.3)). We can then build an algorithm that estimates
the likelihood of the majority voting rule and, accordingly, select the best trading strategy. Since the learning process
happens within the training sample (see Section (2.4.4)), everything is known and we can let the boolean αptq be true
(T) if the estimated direction is correct and false (F) otherwise. Assuming N predictors (or independent variables), we
let nptq be the number of predictors that gives αptq  T. We can therefore estimate n as the minimum n that gives
the maximum number of true ﬂags over the training sample. Thus, when validating and testing the data at a given
time t , we compute all direction triplets, apply the majority voting rule, and infer npt q. Then, the trading rule is as
follows:
