5.1.2.2
Prediction layer
Since machine learning models can learn more than one task, we can use a neural network
(or combine two neural networks) to learn both the future price levels and the asset price directions. It is a simple matter
to modify the above algorithm to forecast price levels or returns. When discussing continuous technical indicators as
input variables, Kara et al. [2011], Patel et al. [2015], among others, considered a three layer ANN model, which
does not account for serially correlated time series. However, we have already presented a framework in Section (3)
that forecast market returns and highlighted its good predictive power on denoised ﬁnancial time series. Since the
continuous technical indicators are mostly ﬁltered ﬁnancial time series, we can use a recurrent neural network (RNN)
model and consider continuous technical indicators to forecast future directions (see Figure ( 17)). The time dependent
direction measures used to assess the predictive power of the algorithm are deﬁned in Appendix (9.4.3).
Figure 18: Predicting with trend continuous data
5.1.2.3
The algorithm
The steps of the algorithm are as follows:
• Preprocess the data
• Denoise the data with PCA, wavelet decomposition, or other ﬁltering tools
• Create the technical indicators in time and space
• Select the best indicators (or Cluster the indicators and stack them)
• Build a model to forecast returns
• Build a model to forecast directions
• Combine the two previous models, or build a model that simultaneously forecast both returns and directions
43
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
• Deﬁne a set of trading rules
• Deﬁne trading strategies and implement an adaptive decision trading system (ADTS)
5.2
Generative networks
We are now going to present a few methods using a generative adversarial network (GAN) models (see Appendix
(19)) to forecast the market prices and their directions.
5.2.1
Combining LSTM and CNN
Zhou et al. [2018] proposed a GAN model using long short-term memory (LSTM) and a convolutional neural network
(CNN) for adversarial training to forecast high-frequency stock market. The method of rolling partition training and
testing set is used to analyse the effect of the model update cycle on the prediction performance. Basic technical index
data was used as input variable.
We let Xt be a vector of basic indicators and Yt be the closing price at time t. We let T  t1, 2, ..., Tu be a set of oneminute interval, where T is the maximum time lag set to 242. Historical data is denoted by X  tX1, X2, ..., XT u
and Y  tY1, Y2, ..., YT u, the goal being to predict YT  1. The generative model G is the LSTM model, and the
output is pYT  1  GpXq. The discriminative model D is the CNN model performing convolution operations on the
