6
Predicting patterns
Even if the market is weakly efﬁcient, in the sense that on average local irregularities naturally disappear, we can still
build a system to identify these hidden short-term irregularities when they occur and generate proﬁtable trades based
on deﬁned trading strategies (see Plummer [1993]). We are going to discuss autonomous pattern generation (case
r2 s in Section (2.4.1)) and their prediction in view of forecasting price trends, where the patterns are distinctive
formations of market prices (see Deﬁnition (11.1)).
6.1
Overview
The stock market provides a lot of ﬁnancial information. In order to make their trading decisions, day traders use
various types of market data, commonly referred to as Level I and Level II market data. Level 3 is the highest level
of quotes provided by a trading service and gives the institution the ability to enter quotes, execute orders and send
information. All three levels of quotes build on top of each other. Basic market data is known as Level I data (bid /
ask price, bid / ask size, last price, last size), while Level II (also known as the order book) provides more information
(highest / lowest bid / ask price, highest / lowest bid / ask size,). When using big data we call these market data features
(or variables). It consists of date, type, completion price, completion amount, opening price, high price, low price,
price just before, accumulated completion amount, accumulated completion price.
As discussed in Section (2.5), machine learning (ML) has been used to analyse and predict stock prices. For instance,
Cao et al.
[2001] used SVM to determine whether the new pattern data belongs to a certain category (or types) of
shapes. Others, such as Kimoto et al. [1990], Kohara et al. [1997] used ANNs to predict daily stock prices in the case
of complex relationships of the variables. However, stock prices consist of several patterns which appear repeatedly
at time intervals (see Appendix (11.1)). Examples of such patterns are consolidation, cup with handle, double bottom,
saucer. The objective is to use observed features to predict the next stock price pattern graph. Several studies focused
on learning traditional shapes deﬁned by stock analysts. For instance, Liu et al. [2007] proposed a method based on
different time window sizes and applied wavelet analysis for identifying and extracting chart patterns from denoised
market data. Jeon et al. [2017] presented a methodology for ﬁnding the optimal historical dataset with similar patterns
according to some algorithms for each stock. Then an ANN model is trained to predict the best stock price. They
aggregated tick-by-tick data to transform non-continuous data into continuous one. A hierarchical clustering algorithm
is used to sort out patterns and a stepwise regression to select the important features. Then an ANN was proposed to
predict market prices. Zhang et al. [2018] proposed a stock price trend prediction system to predict both the price
movement and its interval of growth (or decline) rate within predeﬁned prediction intervals. A Random Forest was
trained to classify clips of stocks into four classes (up, down, ﬂat, and unknown) according to the shapes of their close
prices. They outperformed existing prediction methods in terms of accuracy and return per trade.
It was argued that trend prediction (see Section (4)) is the prediction of stock price ﬂuctuation patterns (see Teixeira
et al. [2010]). Thus, by analysing the properties of stock price ﬂuctuations, and being able of predicting them, we
can forecast the stock price movement. Complex network analysis explain stock market behaviour from a systematic
perspective. This can be achieved with a stock price volatility pattern network (SPVPN). For instance, Huang et al.
[2010] used these networks to assess the properties of market prices on the Shanghai Stock Exchange (SSE) and
predict their trends. The results showed that the networks followed the basic ﬂuctuations exhibited during different
periods. Wang et al. [2016] used these networks to study the properties of crude oil and gasoline in different periods.
Using these properties they predicted oil and gasoline stock price patterns. However, most studies on SPVPN focused
on univariate time series. Cao et al. [2019] proposed a pattern network construction method for multivariate stock
time series.
Since estimating ﬁnancial patterns is concerned with inferring some knowledge about the future trajectory of market
returns, forecasting patterns is a multi-step prediction-learning problem (see Appendix (16.1)). Moreover, singlestep prediction problems generated by a dynamical system are more naturally viewed as multi-step problems. Sutton
47
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
[1988] showed that temporal difference (TD) methods were more efﬁcient than supervised learning methods when
the data sequences were generated by a dynamical system. It is therefore logical to use TD methods rather than
supervised learning when forecasting patterns. Lee [2001] proposed a reinforcement learning model by combining
a TDp0q algorithm with ANN to learn the values of states corresponding to a stock price trend at a given time. The
state-values represent the expected cumulative reward. The performance of the method was evaluated on the Korean
stock market. We modify the TD-procedures and propose some methods for predicting ﬁnancial patterns and assessing
their probabilities.
6.2
Predicting shapes
As discussed in Appendix (11.1), it is assumed that different chart patterns reveal different market trends. For example,
a head-and-shoulder top pattern (see Figure (28)) will most likely rise before coming back to the same level. The main
idea is to use a model to identify chart patterns on the historical time series of an underlying asset. These extracted
patterns from market data are used as training data in a neural network. Once the model has learned these shapes, it
can then be used to predict chart patterns. By successfully predicting chart patterns, a system can implement the right
trading strategy and generate proﬁt.
6.2.1
Some examples
6.2.1.1
Chart pattern prediction
Liu et al. [2007] used different time window sizes, W  tw1, ..., wmu, to locate
the occurrence of a particular pattern, and applied wavelet multiresolution (MR) analysis and radial basis function networks (RBFN) for identifying market irregularities. The trading data contains a set of time data, T  tt1, t2, ..., tnu,
which is divided into temporary subset Tj, for j  1, 2, .... A set P is deﬁned, where P  T, which contains the time
ranges for previously identiﬁed wave patterns. Given a template of chart patterns, the trading data is analysed to see
if it is a predeﬁned pattern. Since the data is noisy, it can not directly be used for comparison. Both the input data
and the template have been denoised with wavelet decomposition (see Appendix (10.2)). Given the window size wi,
wavelet analysis is applied to the subset Tj. The process goes from coarse to ﬁne scales. If a predeﬁned wave pattern
is identiﬁed we add Tj to P, otherwise the process is repeated with ﬁner scale. Eventually, all identiﬁed wave patterns
are stored in P.
Pattern matching (recognition) was performed with a radial basis function network (RBFN) with p nodes in the hidden
layer (since simple MR matching was not efﬁcient). Different RBFNs were used to recognise different patterns at
different resolution level. The input of the network is the wavelet transformed values in a particular resolution. The
input layer consists of past network outputs (feedback effect) and the variables explaining the prediction problem.
Given the past observation sequence tXt, Xt1, ..., Xtn 1u of size n and the explanatory variables tVt,1, ..., Vt,mu,
we want to predict the sequence tXt 1, ..., Xt ku, with predictive step k ¡ 0.
Since there was not enough patterns extracted from the HK stock market (only 64), the authors used the radial deformation technique to generate a total 308 patterns. A quarter of the training set was extracted as validation set. Once
the appropriate settings were obtained, ten different stocks over a ten year period were tested for identiﬁcation and
extraction of the chart patterns. The RBFN was on average 81% accurate. However, the accuracy of the recognition
process was heavily dependent on the resolution level. The problem becomes that of identifying the right resolution
level.
Note, this test was performed on denoised data with high wavelet resolution. Further, once the chart patterns have
been extracted, it remains to test for the prediction of chart patterns in real market environment, assess its exploitation
by a trading strategy, and measure the potential return generated by a trading system.
6.2.1.2
Automated price predicting system
Jeon et al.
[2017] proposed an automated system for short-term
prediction of stock prices. The daily stock prices are predicted. The system includes ﬁve steps:
1. Preprocessing step: The data from the KOSPI started on August 2014 and ended on July 2015. The tick-by-tick
data is aggregated at ﬁve minute intervals to get continuous data.
48
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
2. Searching similar patterns: The length of a pattern is one day and patterns are generated at ﬁve minute intervals
with a sliding window. There are twelve patterns in an hour. Similar patterns are found by comparing historical
patterns with the current pattern. Find all similar patterns for one year using hierarchical clustering algorithm.
3. Feature selection: Given all features, use a stepwise regression to remove insigniﬁcant variables. It uses a
bidirectional elimination approach. It repeatedly add and remove a variable among all the variables, and conduct
regression analysis with the remainder. Use R-square as a selection measure.
4. Prediction model: Use ANN to generate the ﬁnal prediction model. Five hidden layers were used. The output is
a predicted data set.
5. Verify the validity of the model using RMSE as a measure of accuracy. The actual data was compared against
two data values predicted by the model.
6.2.2
Automatic data processing system
Since stock markets carry out huge amount of transactions every day (for instance high frequency trading), unsupervised pattern recognition is becoming more important with increasing processing power of computers (see Wu et al.
[2014]). Zhang et al. [2018] proposed an automatic data processing system to predict the trend of stock prices. It uses
unsupervised pattern recognition methods to automatically generate training samples from raw transaction data. All
patterns are extracted from the stock market and the interested ones are transformed into training samples. Multiple
learning models are trained to meet the prediction goals. The trading frequency chosen is the day and the forecast
duration range from 10 days to 60 days.
As discussed in Appendix ((11.1)), any stock chart may exhibit countless different pattern combinations. To simplify
the search space, the authors focussed on four classes. The four classes considered are: Up (trend), Down (trend),
Flat, Unknown (noisy mean-reverting). There are two subclasses for the Up/Down, the growth (decline) rate (changes
of close prices) p1q within the range r10, 30s% and p2q greater than 30%. Note, relative return with respect to a
benchmark is also considered. It leads to selecting the AbsoluteMode and the RelativeMode.
There are three duration periods:
1. The longest period is the Pattern Duration (PD) (clip): the expected pattern.
2. The Model Duration (MD): all data points are used as training sample. MD is 2
3 of PD.
3. The smallest period is the Test Duration (TD): all data points form an input instance whose labels and their
probabilities will be predicted.
Using a sliding window of predeﬁned size moving with a one-day step, the raw data is divided into pieces (clips) which
are analysed to match predeﬁned patterns. It is based on the (simple) shape of the close prices over a ﬁxed duration
period. The shape of a clip determines the class label of its corresponding training sample. Heuristic algorithms based
on geometry are used to recognise the deﬁned patterns. The training samples are generated according to the recognised
patterns. The method generates as many samples as possible to cover all possible cases. Then ML models (Random
Forest) are trained on these samples.
Observing data at the start of the trading period, the prediction interface predicts the probability of forming a predeﬁned
shape over a ﬁxed duration period. For a given stock, it provides the probability of going up or down with its associated
probability of increase or decrease of the price. The user speciﬁes the historical period under study as well as the
prediction period, and the model provides the prediction results for every stock. Sorting these probabilities, the user
will know the stocks having the highest probability of increase or decrease and generating the highest returns. In
essence, the training sample is a subset of the clip, and the ML model is trained to learn the latter from the former.
Thus, rather than learning St from St1, the system learns a shape covering the data points tSt, St 1, ..., St du from
the sample tStk, Stk 1, ..., St1u, d, k P N, for a clip of size d   k   1.
49
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
In practice most clips belong to the class Unknown so that we get an imbalanced learning problem. To go around, the
authors balanced the number of samples for all classes using the undersampling technique. The features of the training
samples (close, open, high and low) were extended by adding multiple technical indices of each trade day. The feature
selection method is based on the Forward Sequential Search method, which select one among all the candidates to the
current state.
The system was tested on 495 stocks on the Chinese market from January 2010 till the end of September 2016.
Assuming several ﬁxed prediction durations, 70 learning models were considered on 70 data sets. The data was split
in 30{70 predict / train. Excluding feature selection, the average accuracy of all models was 67.5%. The system
performed better for predicting trend in long term duration in the range of 30 to 40 days. The performances were
improved by adding technical indices in the set of features. The system can be reﬁned by adding the subclasses in a
two-stage prediction scheme. In that setting, the performances decreased to an average performance greater than 60%.
6.3
Predicting stock price ﬂuctuations
Since the behaviour of hidden short-term irregularities is non-linear, researchers developed neural networks and other
wavelet frameworks to capture these irregularities (or ﬂuctuations) to forecast market directions. Some studies on
predicting stock price ﬂuctuations focused on assessing volatility patterns of single stocks (see Moghaddam et al.
[2016]). However, Kwon et al.
[2005] showed that price ﬂuctuations in a single stock were not isolated, but were
inﬂuenced by the trends of multiple related stocks. Complex network analysis explain stock market behaviour from a
systematic perspective. Several studies proposed different methods for building complex networks from time series,
such as visibility graphs, recurrence networks (see Marwan et al. [2009], Donner et al. [2010]), correlation networks,
pattern networks (see Wang et al. [2016], [2017]), and K-neighbours networks. The symbolic pattern networks reﬂect
well the degree of correlation and direction of primitive elements in a complex system. In a stock price volatility
pattern network (SPVPN), each volatility pattern is regarded as a network node, and the relationship between patterns
is regarded as a connection between nodes. One infer the properties of stock price ﬂuctuations from the topological
properties of the network. Most studies on SPVPN focused on univariate time series.
6.3.1
Complex network methods
Cao et al.
[2019] considered complex network methods to analyse the stock price ﬂuctuation patterns of the main
US indices. They proposed a pattern network construction method for multivariate stock time series. The volatility
patterns analysed (every thirty days) are: the average strength, average shortest path length, average degree centrality,
and closeness centrality. First, the price volatility patterns of these indices were combined using a coarse-grained
method. Then, these price volatility combination patterns of the main US indices were used as nodes of the network,
and the frequencies and directions of the conversion of the patterns were used as the weights and directions of the
network connections. Then, the directed weighted networks were constructed. The result showed that stock price
volatility pattern networks (SPVPN) were reﬂecting special periods (of sharp ﬂuctuations) on the stock market, and
could be used to identify such periods. We will see in Section (7) that it makes more sense to use the Hurst exponent to
reveal the properties of stock price ﬂuctuations. Thus, these variables (SPVPN) for each combination pattern are used
as input variables for K-nearest neighbours (KNN) and SVM algorithms to predict the next day volatility patterns of a
single stock.
The authors used daily closing prices for each index and computed one-day return, ﬁve-day return, and ﬁve-day
volatility V which they rolled on a sliding window with length thirty days and a one-day time step. This way, we get
a pattern network every thirty days. They computed the average ﬁve-day volatility and obtained the average volatility
for the overall market V
1. From the sign of the ﬁve-day return and its volatility they classiﬁed each stock into four
patterns Si, i  1, 2, 3, 4 ,based on the relation between V and V
1. The patterns of each stock index were combined.
Assuming three indices, we get 64 combination modes. The daily combination patterns are the nodes of the networks
and the edges and weights are determined in time order. Then, the authors computed the characteristic variables for
every network:
