6.4
Using TD to predict patterns
We discussed in Section (3) how to perform a one-point forecast to estimate the level of return, and we presented
in Section (4) different ways of estimating the next direction of market returns. However, in order to devise trading
strategies, traders are more interested in estimating ﬁnancial patterns (see Appendix (11.1)). That is, inferring some
knowledge about the future trajectory of market returns. Forecasting patterns is a multi-step prediction-learning problem (see Appendix (16.1)). Moreover, single-step prediction problems generated by a dynamical system are more
naturally viewed as multi-step problems. Sutton [1988] showed that temporal difference (TD) methods were more
efﬁcient than supervised learning methods when the data sequences were generated by a dynamical system. It is therefore logical to use TD methods rather than supervised learning when forecasting patterns. Lee [2001] proposed a
reinforcement learning model by combining a TDp0q algorithm with ANN to learn the values of states corresponding
to a stock price trend at a given time. The state-values represent the expected cumulative reward. The performance of
the method was evaluated on the Korean stock market.
We brieﬂy discuss the approach proposed by Lee [2001] and, following Sutton [1988], propose some methods for
predicting ﬁnancial patterns and assessing their probabilities.
