6.4.1.2
TD procedures
We let the data sequences be generated by absorbing Markov processes, where the next
state depends only on the current state. They are deﬁned by a set of terminal states T, a set of nonterminal states N,
and a set of transition probabilities pij, i P N, j P N Y T, where each pij is the probability of a transition from state
i to state j, given that the process is in state i. Since it is absorbing, all sequences eventually terminate. Given an
initial state q1, the absorbing Markov process generates the sequence q1, q2, ..., qm 1, where qm 1 P T. The learning
algorithms do not know about the state sequence, but only of a related observation-outcome sequence x1, x2, ..., xm, z.
We follow the notation in Appendix (16.2) and consider a multi-step problem with experience coming in observationoutcome sequences of the form e 
 x1, x2, ..., xm, z

, where each xt is a vector of observable input at time t in the sequence, and z P R is the outcome of that sequence. A training set consists of several sequences tx1,p, x2,p, ..., xm,p, zpu,
for p P r1, Ps. For each such sequence, the learner produces a sequence of predictions P1, P2, ..., Pm, each of which
being an estimate of z. For simplicity, it is assumed that Pt is a function of xt and a vector of weights w, that is,
Pt  Ppxt, wq. The TDpλq procedure in Equation (16.99) is used to update the weights. Following Sutton, we can
use a linear prediction Pt  w  xt, or, a much complex supervised learning such as a deep neural network.
Since the relationship between input and output in stock prices is highly nonlinear, we should consider a multilayer
ANN to represent the prediction Pt  Ppxt, wq. As discussed in Appendix (16), the update equation is not changed
