6.4.4
Predicting values and inferring patterns
More generally, we consider the sequences e deﬁned above where the vector xt contains the standardised market return
pr at time t, and the outcome z P R is a return value at future time T ¡ m. We want to ﬁnd the conditional expectation
of return being equal to z, given that it was in each of the other states. That is, Erz| xks, for k  1, ..., m. For each
such sequence, the learner produces a sequence of predictions P1, P2, ..., Pm, each of which being an estimate of z.
These predictions are the forward price of market returns for the ﬁxed maturity T. As we move in time along the
sequence, we reﬁne our prediction of z based on the path taken by market returns. When at time t of the sequence, we
have already predicted Pt1, Pt2, .... It gives us some information about the market pattern we are in.
As discussed in Section (6.2), different chart patterns reveal different market trends. The main idea is to use a model
to identify chart patterns on the historical time series of an underlying asset and use these patterns as training data in a
