9.4
Accounting for time and earning proﬁts
In traditional time series forecasting, the criteria for assessing model performance are error functions based on the
goodness of ﬁt of target and predicted values. Since neural networks are similar to conventional regression estima80
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
tors, except for their nonlinearity, error functions are also used to judge the goodness of ﬁt of the model. However,
normalised mean square error (NMSE) and other error functions are not the most appropriate measures in ﬁnance.
9.4.1
Time factor
For instance, weighting all data equally (ordinary leasy squares) are less accurate than discounted least squares, which
weight the most recent data more heavily (see Makridakis et al. [1982]). Incorporating time factor to neural networks,
Refenes et al. [1997] proposed the discounted least squares (DLS) neural network model. Assuming a feed-forward
network with n input and a single output unit (m  1) with k hidden layers, we let the ordinary least-squares criterion
of the network be given by
ELS  1
