10.1.4
The PCA
Principal component analysis (PCA) is a statistical procedure that uses an orthogonal transformation to convert a set
of observations of possibly correlated variables into a set of values of linearly uncorrelated variables called principal
components. PCA aims at constructing a low-dimensional representation of the data while maintaining the maximal
variance and covariance structure of the data (see Jolliffe [1986]). It is the simplest of the true eigenvector-based
multivariate analyses. If a multivariate dataset is visualised as a set of coordinates in a high-dimensional data space (1
85
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
axis per variable), PCA can supply the user with a lower-dimensional picture, a projection of this object when viewed
from its most informative viewpoint. It is closely related to factor analysis, which incorporates more domain speciﬁc
assumptions about the underlying structure and solves eigenvectors of a slightly different matrix.
10.1.4.1
Description
Let X P Rn be a random vector. We want to ﬁnd the single direction capturing as much as
possible of the variance of X. That is, we want the direction p P Rn such that }p}  1, so as to maximise V arppJXq.
Theorem 10.4 The solution to this optimisation problem is to make p the principal eigenvector of CovpXq.
Again, we let µ  ErXs and S  CovpXq. For any p P Rn, the projection pJX has mean ErpJXs  pJµ and
variance
V arppJXq  Er
 pJX  pJµ
2s  ErpJpX  µqpX  µqJps  pJSP
By Theorem (10.2), this is maximised, over all unit-length p, when p is the principal eigenvector of S.
Likewise, the k-dimensional subspace capturing as much as possible of the variance of X is simply the subspace
spanned by the top k eigenvectors of CovpXq. If we call them u1, ..., uk, the projection onto these eigenvectors is
called Principal Component Analysis (PCA). It can be used to reduce the dimension of the data from n to k as follow
• compute the mean µ and covariance matrix S of the data X.
• compute the top k eigenvectors u1, ..., uk of S.
• project X Ñ P JX, where P J is the k  n matrix whose rows are u1, ..., uk.
10.1.4.2
Afﬁne approximation
Another property of PCA is that it is the k-dimensional afﬁne subspace best approximating X, in the sense that the expected squared distance from X to the subspace is minimised. Formally, a kdimensional afﬁne subspace is given by a displacement p0 P Rn and a set of orthonormal basis vectors p1, ..., pk P Rn.
The subspace itself is then tp0 α1p1 ... αkpk : αi P Ru. The projection of X P Rn onto this subspace is P JX p0,
where P J is the k  n matrix whose rows are p1, ..., pk. Thus, the expected squared distance from X to this subspace
is E}X  pP JX   p0q}2. We can ﬁnd the subspace for which this is minimised.
Theorem 10.5 Let µ and S denote the mean and covariance of X, respectively. The solution of this optimisation
problem is to choose p1, ..., pk to be the top k eigenvectors of S and to set p0  pI  P Jqµ.
Fixing any matrix P, the choice p0 minimising E}X  pP JX   p0q}2 is p0  ErX  P T Xs  pI  P Jqµ. The
