11.6.2
Trading styles
We need to consider a particular trading style in order to determine the optimal time frame for MAC strategies. For
examples:
• Day trading: For day traders, who hold positions for a few hours or less, shorter time frames are more suitable.
A combination of 5, 8, and 13-bar simple moving averages (SMAs) can be effective for day trading strategies.
• Swing trading: Swing traders, who hold positions for a few days to a few weeks, can use moving average
crossovers to enter trades. Commonly used time frames for swing trading include 20-day, 50-day, and 200-day
SMAs.
• Long-term investing: For long-term investors, longer time frames are more appropriate. Commonly used periods
for creating moving average lines include 50-day, 100-day, and 200-day moving averages.
Day trading require continuous feedback on short-term price action to make very fast buy and sell decisions. One
approach is to use intraday bars wrapped in multiple moving averages, allowing quick analysis that highlights current
risks (as well as the most advantageous entries and exits). The 5  8  13-bar simple moving averages (SMAs)
offer relatively strong inputs for day trading strategies. To improve results, one should also look at the slopes of
the MAs which reﬂect subtle shifts in short-term momentum. Increases in observed momentum (acceleration) offer
buying opportunities for day traders, while decreases in momentum (deceleration) may signal timely exits. Trend-less
markets and periods of high volatility will force the 5  8  13-bar SMAs into large-scale whipsaws (oscillations),
with horizontal orientation and frequent crossovers telling observant traders to to stand aside. In that case, the slope
tends to near zero, indicating that intraday trending is weak and proﬁtable opportunities are limited. In fact, trading
ranges expand in volatile markets and contract in trend-less markets.
When performing day trading with RSI, we choose between 6 and 9 periods, so that more overbought and oversold
signals are generated. Ideally, these levels should correspond with support and resistance levels. Thus, in a ranging
market, buy orders will be placed off a support area when the RSI reading is below 30, whereas sell orders will be
123
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
placed off a resistance area when the RSI reading is above 70.
In trending markets, the RSI setting can be even lower p2  6q periods, so that we can identify the best value areas to
enter the market in the direction of the dominant trend. For instance, in an uptrend, we will look to enter buy trades
after a pullback that will be indicated by an oversold RSI reading. Similarly, in a downtrend, sell trades will be placed
after a retracement that will be highlighted by an overbought RSI reading.
Since a lower setting will generate numerous overbought and oversold signals, it is important to combine RSI and
other technical indicators, such as moving averages (MAs), in trending markets so as to ﬁlter out the best quality
signals when day trading. MAs will conﬁrm the trend and also provide possible retracement target levels where RSI
overbought and oversold signals can conﬁrm solid trading opportunities.
11.6.3
Some advantages and drawbacks
Moving average trading indicators help us trace the price changes and ﬂuctuations so that we can take the right
decisions regarding to the trading position. Using MA indicators, we can identify an uptrend (the price moves above
the moving average) or a downtrend (the price moves below the moving average). They are used to predict the price
change and the change in the trend of the ﬁnancial market. Considering other factors such as the length of the trading
period, moving average crossover, etc., we can ﬁnd out the trading positions, the entry points when the prices are
strongly trending. Further, MAs can be used as support/resistance points. The MAs indicator are responsive to new
and updated information which means better predictions.
Some drawback of moving averages are:
• Lagging indicators: They provide signals after the trend has already begun, which can result in missed opportunities and false signals.
• Whipsaw markets: In markets with choppy price action and no clear trend, MAs can generate a large number of
false signals, leading to losses.
• Slow-moving: MAs are slow-moving indicators, which means they may not respond quickly enough to sudden
changes in market conditions.
• Not suitable for all market conditions: MAC strategies may work well in trending markets but may be less
effective in range-bound or volatile markets (generating multiple trend reversals or trade signals). Adjusting the
time frame can help with this problem.
To maximise the effectiveness of MACs, traders should use them in conjunction with other technical indicators and
fundamental analysis. This way, they can conﬁrm signals and make informed trading decisions.
