Quantitative Analytics
Figure 33: MACD histogram.
Some possible use of the histogram are as follows:
• Positive or negative crossovers: If MACD line is above the signal line, the histogram will be above the MACD’s
baseline (or zero line), otherwise if it is below, the histogram will be below the MACD’s baseline. Zeroes in the
histogram occur when the MACD line crosses the signal line above or below.
• Identify strength: The magnitude of height is directly proportional to the spread between the MACD and signal
lines. When the spread between the two lines increases, the histogram becomes taller or deeper, depending on its
direction. When the opposite scenario occurs (when the two lines draw closer),the histogram becomes shorter.
• Identify bullish (when MACD rises above the signal line) or bearish (when MACD falls below the signal line)
momentum, and possibly identify overbought/oversold signals. MACD functions on a relative basis so that we
should focus on the level and direction of the MACD/signal lines compared with preceding price movements in
the security at hand. Peaks and troughs in the histogram indicate when a burst of bearish or bullish momentum
is losing strength, and the curve is likely to return to its mean.
• Divergence: When MACD forms highs or lows that that exceed the corresponding highs and lows on the price,
it is called a divergence. A bullish divergence appears when MACD forms two rising lows that correspond with
two falling lows on the price. This is a valid bullish signal when the long-term trend is still positive. Similarly,
when MACD forms a series of two falling highs that correspond with two rising highs on the price, a bearish
divergence has been formed. A bearish divergence appearing during a long-term bearish trend is considered
conﬁrmation that the trend is likely to continue.
• Rapid rises or falls: When MACD rises or falls rapidly (the shorter-term moving average pulls away from the
longer-term moving average), it is a signal that the security is overbought or oversold and will soon return
to normal levels. This analysis can be combined with the RSI or other TIs to verify overbought or oversold
conditions.
• Predict change in trend: We can use MACD histogram to predict when a change in trend will occur. In that
case, it makes MACD a leading indicator. This is done by computing the slope of the histogram. From the slope
126
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
of the histogram, one can get the most common signal. For example, when the current bar is higher than the
preceding bar, the slope is up and one can go long. A slant divergence is a slope that is capable of creating a
divergence without well-deﬁned peaks/troughs.
For simplicity, we consider the case where we buy (long) the security when the MACD line crosses above the signal
line, and sell (short) the security when the MACD line crosses below the signal line. That is, given some ϵ,
• Go long when the MACD histogram turns up below zero, Histt ¡ ϵ.
• Go short when MACD histogram turns down above zero, Histt   ϵ.
• Otherwise we don’t trade.
