11.6.4.5
The RSI
The RSI indicator is given in Equation (11.32). Since the main signal the RSI oscillator deﬁnes
overbought and oversold price ranges, we consider the overbought or oversold strategy. When the RSI line goes above
the level 70 or below the level 30, it signals that market is overbought/oversold, respectively. The rules for opening
and closing positions based on the RSI signals are:
• If the RSI line crosses the level 30 from above (or ﬁrst from above and then from below), a long position (buy)
is opened. Exit on opposite indicator’s signal. Unwind the position if the RSI line crosses the level 70 from
below (or ﬁrst from below and then from above).
• If the RSI line crosses the level 70 from below (or ﬁrst from below and then from above), a short position (sell)
is opened. Exit on opposite indicator’s signal. Unwind the position if the RSI line crosses the level 30 from
above (or ﬁrst from above and then from below).
11.6.5
Trading rules
The Triple Moving Average (TMA) strategy has the following trading rules (See Faith [?]):
1. Long position
• Enter long rules:
– Open a long position when the MAtpNSq crosses over the MAtpNMq, and
– Both the MAtpNSq and MAtpNMq are above the MAtpNLq.
• Exit long rules:
127
Electronic copy available at: https://ssrn.com/abstract=3232143

Quantitative Analytics
– Sell the long position when the MAtpNSq crosses below the MAtpNMq.
2. Short position
• Enter short rules:
– Open a short position when the MAtpNSq crosses under the MAtpNMq, and
– Both the MAtpNSq and MAtpNMq are below the MAtpNLq.
• Exit short rules: Cover a short position when the MAtpNSq crosses above the MAtpNMq.
Note, the TMA strategy only trades when two moving averages are on the same side of the MAtpNLq. Further, even
though the TMA strategy lacks of stop-loss orders, there is an implicit stop. When prices move against our position,
sooner or later the short MA crosses the middle MA and the strategy generates its exit signal. However, the exit time
is random so that the price level is unknown. Hence, we must impose a global stop loss. At last, the TMAverage
strategy underperforms signiﬁcantly when prices move sideways. One solution is to ﬁlter out those low-probability
trades (with speciﬁc technical indicators).
The template for the TMA strategy is:
1. Deﬁne strategy settings
2. Calculate strategy values
3. Determine long trading conditions
4. Code short trading conditions
5. Output strategy data
6. Submit entry orders
7. Submit exit orders
