13.3.2
The procedure
Random forests (RF) is an extension of bagging incorporating randomised feature selection. During the construction
of a component decision tree, at each step of split selection (deterministic), RF ﬁrst randomly selects a subset of features, and then carries out the conventional split selection procedure within the selected feature subset. That is, RF use
a modiﬁed tree learning algorithm that selects, at each candidate split in the learning process, a random subset of the
features. It is called feature bagging. Typically, for a classiﬁcation problem with p features, ?p features are used in
each split.
The parameter K controls the incorporation of randomness. When K equals the total number of features, the constructed decision tree is identical to the traditional deterministic decision tree, but when K  1, a feature will be
selected randomly. Breiman [2001] suggested K  log p, the logarithm of the number of features.
